<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Zápolya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1487–1540</span> · Also known as John I of Hungary, John Szapolyai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Zápolya was Voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> from 1510 and, after the death of Louis II at Mohács in 1526, was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> by the nobles as John I. A rival party elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand of Habsburg</a>, and Zápolya turned to the Ottoman sultan Suleiman for support. The kingdom was divided, and the Treaty of Nagyvárad in 1538 recognised his rule in the east, which became the Eastern Hungarian Kingdom and later Transylvania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-zapolya.jpg" alt="John Zápolya" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Erhard Schön (1491-1542) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Szapolyai_J%C3%A1nos_fametszet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
